{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We have a problem: count binary words of length 12 that do not contain any forbidden substring \"000\" or \"111\". So we need to count number of binary strings of length 12 avoiding runs of 3 zeros or 3 ones. This is a classic combinatorics / automaton counting problem. We can use recurrence or dynamic programming. Let's compute.\n\nWe need to count sequences of length n over alphabet {0,1} with no run of 3 identical bits.\n\nWe can model states based on last two bits? Actually we need to know the current run length of the last symbol. So state: (last symbol, run length). For run length 1 or 2. So states: (0,1), (0,2), (1,1), (1,2). For length 0 we can start with either 0 or 1. Let's compute DP.\n\nLet a_n0 = number of strings of length n ending with a run of 1 zero (i.e., last bit 0, run length 1). a_n1 = ending with run length 2 zeros. Similarly b_n0 = ending with run length 1 ones, b_n1 = ending with run length 2 ones.\n\nRecurrence: For n=1: a_1_0 = 1 (string \"0\"), a_1_1=0, b_1_0=1 (\"1\"), b_1_1=0.\n\nFor n>1: To get a_n_0 (ending with single 0), previous bit must be 1 (any run length). So a_n_0 = b_{n-1}_0 + b_{n-1}_1. Similarly a_n_1 (ending with two zeros) comes from previous state a_{n-1}_0 (single 0) appended 0: a_n_1 = a_{n-1}_0. Similarly b_n_0 = a_{n-1}_0 + a_{n-1}_1. b_n_1 = b_{n-1}_0.\n\nWe can compute iteratively up to n=12.\n\nLet's do table.\n\nn=1:\na0=1, a1=0, b0=1, b1=0. Total=2.\n\nn=2:\na0 = b0 + b1 = 1+0=1\na1 = a0_prev = 1\nb0 = a0_prev + a1_prev = 1+0=1\nb1 = b0_prev = 1\nCheck: Wait b1 = b0_prev? Actually b1 = b_{n-1}_0. For n=2, b1 = b0_prev = 1. Yes.\n\nSo n=2 totals: a0=1,a1=1,b0=1,b1=1 => total 4. All strings of length 2 allowed (since no 3 consecutive). Good.\n\nn=3:\nCompute using previous (n=2) values: a0_prev=1,a1_prev=1,b0_prev=1,b1_prev=1.\n\na0 = b0_prev + b1_prev = 1+1=2\na1 = a0_prev = 1\nb0 = a0_prev + a1_prev = 1